  "post": {
    "caption": "The Met's goldsmith got his halo later. In 1449, Petrus Christus painted A Goldsmith in His Shop. The goldsmith weighs a wedding ring for a richly dressed couple. A marriage belt lies across the shop ledge. His shelves hold pearls and coral. Among them are fossil shark teeth, believed to protect against poison. A small round mirror shows two men in the street. For years, he was called Saint Eligius, the goldsmiths' saint. A 1990s restoration removed the halo, added by another hand. In 1998, scholars argued he lacks a saint's attributes. The Met keeps it in its Robert Lehman Collection.",
